# Caddyfile.example — Local HTTPS reverse proxy for jira_workload (Option B)
#
# This file enables HTTPS on localhost:4443 and proxies to the jira_workload
# backend container, satisfying Atlassian's HTTPS callback URL requirement
# without an internet tunnel.
#
# Setup (one-time per developer machine):
#   1. Install mkcert and create a local CA:
#        macOS:   brew install mkcert && mkcert -install
#        Linux:   sudo apt install mkcert && mkcert -install
#        Windows: choco install mkcert; mkcert -install  (elevated PowerShell)
#
#   2. Generate a certificate for localhost:
#        mkcert localhost 127.0.0.1 ::1
#      This produces: localhost+2.pem  and  localhost+2-key.pem
#
#   3. Move the certificate files into the certs/ directory:
#        mkdir -p certs
#        mv localhost+2.pem     certs/
#        mv localhost+2-key.pem certs/
#
#   4. Copy this file to Caddyfile (no .example extension):
#        cp Caddyfile.example Caddyfile
#
#   5. Uncomment the caddy service in podman-compose.yml (see comment block there).
#
#   6. Set in .env:
#        ATLASSIAN_REDIRECT_URI=https://localhost:4443/oauth/callback
#
#   7. Register https://localhost:4443/oauth/callback in the Atlassian Developer Console.
#
#   8. Restart the Podman stack: ./start.sh  (or .\start.ps1 on Windows)
#
# See OAUTH_SETUP.md § 2a Option B for the full guide.
# The certs/ directory is gitignored — never commit private key files.

https://localhost:4443 {
    tls /certs/localhost+2.pem /certs/localhost+2-key.pem
    reverse_proxy app:4000
}
